The pyramid of biomass in the sea, starting with phytoplankton, is drawn as three stacked bars:
- at the base, the **producers (PP): phytoplankton**, the narrowest bar;
- above it, the **primary consumers (PC): zooplankton**, a wider bar;
- at the top, the **secondary consumers (SC): fishes**, the widest bar.

The pyramid is **inverted**. The biomass of fishes is much more than that of the zooplankton and phytoplankton below them, so the bars widen towards the top. The small standing crop of phytoplankton can support a larger biomass of consumers because phytoplankton reproduce rapidly and have a short life span, so a small biomass present at any one time is replaced quickly.

The pyramid of numbers for this food chain is drawn as four stacked bars, each labelled with its trophic level and organism:
- at the base, **producer (PP): one very big tree**, a narrow bar, because there is only one tree;
- above it, **primary consumers (PC): insects**, the widest bar, because a large population of insects feeds on the single tree;
- above that, **secondary consumers (SC): small birds**, a narrower bar, because fewer small birds feed on the insects;
- at the top, **tertiary consumers (TC): big birds**, the narrowest bar.

Since the producer level is narrower than the level above it, the pyramid is **not upright**; it is spindle-shaped (partly inverted), widest at the primary-consumer level.

(i) The diagrammatic sectional view of a seminiferous tubule (enlarged) shows:
- at the outer wall (basement membrane), the **spermatogonia**, with the interstitial region and blood vessels outside the tubule;
- moving towards the lumen, the **primary spermatocytes**, then the **secondary spermatocytes**, then the **spermatids**;
- in the lumen, the **sperms** with their tails, their heads embedded among the cells;
- large **Sertoli cells** lying among the germ cells, reaching from the wall towards the lumen.

The labels asked for:
- (a) the cell that undergoes **spermiogenesis** is the **spermatid**, which is transformed into a spermatozoon (sperm);
- (b) the cell that nourishes the male gametes is the **Sertoli cell**, which provides nutrition to the germ cells;
- (c) the **primary spermatocyte** undergoes **meiosis I**, forming two secondary spermatocytes, and the **secondary spermatocyte** undergoes **meiosis II**, forming spermatids.

(ii)
- **Seminal plasma** is the fluid made of the secretions of the male accessory glands: the paired **seminal vesicles**, the **prostate** and the paired **bulbourethral glands**.
- It is rich in **fructose, calcium and certain enzymes** (any two). The secretions of the bulbourethral glands also help in the lubrication of the penis.
- Seminal plasma **along with the sperms** constitutes the **semen**. So seminal plasma is only the fluid part, while semen is the seminal plasma plus the sperms.
